.104421","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,11]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,11]]},"container-title":["Artificial Intelligence"]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.artint.2023.103963","title":["Discovering agents"],"author":[{"given":"Zachary","family":"Kenton","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Ramana","family":"Kumar","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Sebastian","family":"Farquhar","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Jonathan","family":"Richens","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Matt","family":"MacDermott","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Tom","family":"Everitt","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.artint.2023.103963","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2023,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2023,9]]},"container-title":["Artificial Intelligence"]},{"DOI":"10.5220\/0014334300004052","title":["Using Spatiotemporal Changes in Memory as a Form of Attention"],"author":[{"given":"Fernando","family":"Duarte","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"IEETA, DETI, LASI, University of Aveiro, Aveiro, Portugal"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Nuno","family":"Lau","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"IEETA, DETI, LASI, University of Aveiro, Aveiro, Portugal"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Artur","family":"Pereira","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"IEETA, DETI, LASI, University of Aveiro, Aveiro, Portugal"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Lu\u00eds","family":"Reis","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Faculty of Engineering, Department of Informatics Engineering, University of Porto, Porto, Portugal"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.5220\/0014334300004052","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"container-title":["Proceedings of the 18th International Conference on Agents and Artificial Intelligence"]},{"DOI":"10.1017\/9781009258227.029","title":["References"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1017\/9781009258227.029","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2023,7,13]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2023,7,13]]},"container-title":["Artificial Intelligence"]},{"DOI":"10.1017\/9781009258227.031","title":["Index"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1017\/9781009258227.031","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2023,7,13]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2023,7,13]]},"container-title":["Artificial Intelligence"]},{"DOI":"10.1017\/9781009258227.015","title":["Causality"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1017\/9781009258227.015","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2023,7,13]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2023,7,13]]},"container-title":["Artificial Intelligence"]},{"DOI":"10.1017\/9781009258227.001","title":["Preface"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1017\/9781009258227.001","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2023,7,13]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2023,7,13]]},"container-title":["Artificial Intelligence"]},{"DOI":"10.51628\/001c.94404","title":["Artificial intelligence is algorithmic mimicry: why artificial \u201cagents\u201d are not (and won\u2019t be) proper agents"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-2568-2103","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Johannes","family":"Jaeger","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"id":[{"id":"https:\/\/ror.org\/03prydq77","id-type":"ROR","asserted-by":"publisher"}],"name":"University of Vienna","department":["Dept of Philosophy"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.51628\/001c.94404","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2024,2,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2024,2,27]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>What is the prospect of developing artificial general intelligence (AGI)? I investigate this question by systematically comparing living and algorithmic systems, with a special focus on the notion of \u201cagency.\u201d There are three fundamental differences to consider: (1) Living systems are autopoietic, that is, self-manufacturing, and therefore able to set their own intrinsic goals, while algorithms exist in a computational environment with target functions that are both provided by an external agent. (2) Living systems are embodied in the sense that there is no separation between their symbolic and physical aspects, while algorithms run on computational architectures that maximally isolate software from hardware. (3) Living systems experience a large world, in which most problems are ill defined (and not all definable), while algorithms exist in a small world, in which all problems are well defined. These three differences imply that living and algorithmic systems have very different capabilities and limitations. In particular, it is extremely unlikely that true AGI (beyond mere mimicry) can be developed in the current algorithmic framework of AI research. Consequently, discussions about the proper development and deployment of algorithmic tools should be shaped around the dangers and opportunities of current narrow AI, not the extremely unlikely prospect of the emergence of true agency in artificial systems.<\/jats:p>","container-title":["Neurons, Behavior, Data analysis, and Theory"]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/qpain69676.2026.11546218","title":["Lightweight Local Market Protocols for Transactive EV Charging with Degradation-Aware Bidding"],"author":[{"given":"P.","family":"Muthukumar","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Saveetha School of Engineering, Saveetha Institute of Medical and Technical Sciences, Saveetha University,Department of EEE,Chennai,India,602105"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.or